1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,871
It's 8pm.

2
00:00:01,289 --> 00:00:05,085
You flop onto your bed, pull out your phone, and open your favourite app.

3
00:00:05,503 --> 00:00:06,792
And here's the wild part.

4
00:00:07,279 --> 00:00:10,937
At that same second, half your city is doing the exact same thing.

5
00:00:11,505 --> 00:00:13,479
Millions of taps, all at once.

6
00:00:13,967 --> 00:00:14,663
And the app?

7
00:00:14,965 --> 00:00:15,906
It just works.

8
00:00:16,324 --> 00:00:18,367
No crash, no spinning wheel.

9
00:00:18,785 --> 00:00:21,212
Ever stopped to wonder how that's even possible?

10
00:00:21,781 --> 00:00:25,345
Stick around, because by the end of this video you'll understand it completely.

11
00:00:25,763 --> 00:00:27,226
Not by memorising anything.

12
00:00:27,586 --> 00:00:28,897
By building one yourself.

13
00:00:29,385 --> 00:00:31,440
Meet our project for the whole series.

14
00:00:31,742 --> 00:00:32,938
A tiny photo app.

15
00:00:33,356 --> 00:00:34,819
We'll call it baby Instagram.

16
00:00:35,237 --> 00:00:36,374
It does two things.

17
00:00:36,479 --> 00:00:37,477
You post a photo.

18
00:00:37,779 --> 00:00:38,801
Your friends see it.

19
00:00:39,288 --> 00:00:40,368
That's the entire app.

20
00:00:40,856 --> 00:00:44,165
And it's all we need to learn how the big ones work.

21
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:47,927
Hold on to that 8pm picture.

22
00:00:48,589 --> 00:00:50,458
A whole city, tapping at once.

23
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:51,956
And nothing breaks.

24
00:00:52,444 --> 00:00:55,160
That isn't luck, and it certainly isn't magic.

25
00:00:55,927 --> 00:01:00,095
Somebody sat down, long before launch, and planned for that crowd.

26
00:01:00,756 --> 00:01:03,555
That invisible plan is system design.

27
00:01:04,217 --> 00:01:07,734
And it might be the most useful thing you'll ever learn as an engineer.

28
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,332
So let's get the definition out of the way, in plain English.

29
00:01:13,994 --> 00:01:19,683
System design is planning how the pieces of your app fit together, before you write a single line of code.

30
00:01:20,344 --> 00:01:22,608
The easiest way to picture it is building a house.

31
00:01:22,968 --> 00:01:25,209
Writing code is laying the bricks.

32
00:01:25,569 --> 00:01:27,194
It's the actual construction.

33
00:01:27,612 --> 00:01:29,284
System design is the blueprint.

34
00:01:29,644 --> 00:01:30,817
Where do the rooms go?

35
00:01:31,084 --> 00:01:32,245
Where do the pipes run?

36
00:01:32,732 --> 00:01:37,399
You draw that first, because nobody builds a house and figures out the bathroom afterwards.

37
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:39,453
Right.

38
00:01:39,871 --> 00:01:41,844
Let's actually design our baby Instagram.

39
00:01:42,262 --> 00:01:44,039
And we're keeping it tiny on purpose.

40
00:01:44,341 --> 00:01:45,385
One simple screen.

41
00:01:45,687 --> 00:01:47,429
A feed, and a button to post.

42
00:01:48,091 --> 00:01:54,905
Here's the secret: the small version teaches you the exact same ideas as the giant version, just without the headache.

43
00:01:55,567 --> 00:01:56,856
So, two jobs.

44
00:01:56,972 --> 00:01:58,075
One, post a photo.

45
00:01:58,644 --> 00:02:00,176
Two, see your friends' feed.

46
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:02,998
Now the big idea.

47
00:02:03,416 --> 00:02:06,644
The one that, once it clicks, you'll start seeing everywhere.

48
00:02:07,875 --> 00:02:12,588
Almost every app you have ever used is really just three pieces, talking to each other.

49
00:02:13,006 --> 00:02:14,481
The first piece is the client.

50
00:02:15,909 --> 00:02:17,557
That's a fancy word for your phone.

51
00:02:17,975 --> 00:02:19,299
The second is the server.

52
00:02:20,530 --> 00:02:27,879
That's the app's brain, running on a powerful computer in some data centre, maybe a thousand kilometres away.

53
00:02:29,307 --> 00:02:30,735
The third is the database.

54
00:02:31,222 --> 00:02:35,680
Picture a giant, very organised filing cabinet, where every photo actually lives.

55
00:02:36,447 --> 00:02:38,107
Client, server, database.

56
00:02:38,525 --> 00:02:41,985
Hold on to those three, every episode after this is built on them.

57
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:45,695
So what do they actually do all day?

58
00:02:46,264 --> 00:02:47,669
One thing, again and again.

59
00:02:48,029 --> 00:02:49,480
Your phone asks for something.

60
00:02:49,840 --> 00:02:51,210
Show me the latest photos.

61
00:02:51,872 --> 00:02:53,253
That little ask has a name.

62
00:02:53,485 --> 00:02:54,797
It's called a request.

63
00:02:55,366 --> 00:02:58,860
The server hears it, gets what's needed, and sends it back.

64
00:02:59,627 --> 00:03:01,333
That return trip is a response.

65
00:03:01,751 --> 00:03:03,655
Picture asking a librarian for a book.

66
00:03:04,073 --> 00:03:04,665
You ask.

67
00:03:04,770 --> 00:03:05,908
They walk to the shelf.

68
00:03:06,210 --> 00:03:07,173
They bring it back.

69
00:03:07,591 --> 00:03:11,771
You're the client, the librarian is the server, and the shelf is the database.

70
00:03:12,258 --> 00:03:14,128
One request, one response.

71
00:03:14,696 --> 00:03:17,204
That's quite literally every app you've ever opened.

72
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:21,496
Let's slow it right down and follow one single tap.

73
00:03:21,984 --> 00:03:23,400
You tap to open your feed.

74
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:25,815
Your phone sends a request to the server.

75
00:03:26,233 --> 00:03:27,348
That's the first hop.

76
00:03:27,916 --> 00:03:32,142
The server doesn't keep the photos itself, so it fetches them from the database.

77
00:03:33,199 --> 00:03:34,058
Second hop.

78
00:03:34,627 --> 00:03:35,938
Then it all travels back.

79
00:03:36,240 --> 00:03:38,423
Database, to server, to your screen.

80
00:03:38,841 --> 00:03:40,199
All in a fraction of a second.

81
00:03:40,687 --> 00:03:42,754
And this is the heart of the whole episode.

82
00:03:43,322 --> 00:03:46,051
That exact path is hiding inside every app you use.

83
00:03:46,469 --> 00:03:48,535
Once you've seen it, you can't unsee it.

84
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:52,997
So how do real engineers design this?

85
00:03:53,485 --> 00:03:54,785
Do they just start typing?

86
00:03:55,272 --> 00:03:56,224
Not a chance.

87
00:03:56,584 --> 00:03:58,744
They follow the same four steps, every time.

88
00:03:59,638 --> 00:04:01,182
Step one, requirements.

89
00:04:01,542 --> 00:04:03,574
What are we building, and who for?

90
00:04:04,468 --> 00:04:06,464
Step two, high-level design.

91
00:04:06,766 --> 00:04:07,799
The big boxes.

92
00:04:08,566 --> 00:04:10,783
Step three, core components.

93
00:04:11,143 --> 00:04:12,467
Zooming into each box.

94
00:04:12,768 --> 00:04:14,162
Step four, scale.

95
00:04:14,649 --> 00:04:15,741
Surviving a crowd.

96
00:04:16,042 --> 00:04:17,331
And here's the fun part.

97
00:04:17,691 --> 00:04:20,582
We're going to run all four, right now, on baby Instagram.

98
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:23,778
Step one.

99
00:04:24,265 --> 00:04:25,125
Requirements.

100
00:04:25,786 --> 00:04:29,978
A serious-sounding word for a simple question: what should the app do?

101
00:04:30,396 --> 00:04:31,986
It should let you post a photo.

102
00:04:32,404 --> 00:04:33,983
And see your friends' feed.

103
00:04:34,285 --> 00:04:35,376
Easy so far.

104
00:04:36,142 --> 00:04:38,290
But here's the bit beginners rush past.

105
00:04:38,592 --> 00:04:39,788
For how many people?

106
00:04:40,554 --> 00:04:43,828
Because ten friends and ten million users are not the same project.

107
00:04:44,397 --> 00:04:45,303
Not even close.

108
00:04:45,872 --> 00:04:49,657
Same app on the screen, completely different problem underneath.

109
00:04:50,225 --> 00:04:51,247
One's a footbridge.

110
00:04:51,735 --> 00:04:53,325
The other's a national highway.

111
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:55,697
Step two.

112
00:04:55,859 --> 00:04:56,927
High-level design.

113
00:04:57,287 --> 00:04:59,249
Big name, gentle idea.

114
00:04:59,818 --> 00:05:01,664
You draw the boxes, and connect them.

115
00:05:02,233 --> 00:05:03,951
Here's our phone, the client.

116
00:05:04,253 --> 00:05:05,205
Here's the server.

117
00:05:05,623 --> 00:05:07,481
And the database, all wired together.

118
00:05:07,899 --> 00:05:09,013
That's the whole move.

119
00:05:09,675 --> 00:05:12,183
This bird's-eye picture is your high-level design.

120
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:14,824
Step three.

121
00:05:15,312 --> 00:05:16,287
Core components.

122
00:05:16,775 --> 00:05:18,331
Now we zoom inside one box.

123
00:05:18,632 --> 00:05:23,555
The boxes and how they connect, that wide view, is high-level design.

124
00:05:24,124 --> 00:05:24,867
HLD.

125
00:05:25,006 --> 00:05:25,622
The map.

126
00:05:26,109 --> 00:05:31,415
But how one single photo gets stored inside the database, that close-up, is low-level design.

127
00:05:32,077 --> 00:05:32,739
LLD.

128
00:05:33,041 --> 00:05:33,923
The street view.

129
00:05:34,283 --> 00:05:35,955
Same system, two zoom levels.

130
00:05:36,315 --> 00:05:38,056
Today, we're staying up on the map.

131
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:41,393
And now, step four.

132
00:05:41,811 --> 00:05:42,380
Scale.

133
00:05:42,868 --> 00:05:44,412
This is where it gets really fun.

134
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:49,149
Picture ten million people opening baby Instagram at 9pm.

135
00:05:49,718 --> 00:05:50,983
That one little server?

136
00:05:51,645 --> 00:05:52,469
It melts.

137
00:05:52,771 --> 00:05:55,372
Think of one ticket counter facing an entire stadium.

138
00:05:55,859 --> 00:05:57,287
So we add more servers.

139
00:05:57,856 --> 00:05:59,749
A whole row of them, splitting the crowd.

140
00:06:00,317 --> 00:06:02,152
That trick is called scaling out.

141
00:06:02,639 --> 00:06:04,276
And the photos everyone wants?

142
00:06:05,043 --> 00:06:10,105
We keep copies close by, in a cache, so we're not bothering the database every time.

143
00:06:10,592 --> 00:06:12,473
More servers, plus a cache.

144
00:06:13,042 --> 00:06:15,178
And just like that, green lights.

145
00:06:15,480 --> 00:06:16,548
We're healthy again.

146
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:22,181
But here's the move that makes you sound like a real engineer.

147
00:06:23,075 --> 00:06:24,875
You don't build all of that on day one.

148
00:06:25,142 --> 00:06:26,012
Start simple.

149
00:06:26,430 --> 00:06:27,255
One server.

150
00:06:27,824 --> 00:06:28,567
Ship it.

151
00:06:28,985 --> 00:06:31,051
Watch what real people actually do with it.

152
00:06:31,469 --> 00:06:34,394
Then add a new piece only when something actually breaks.

153
00:06:34,963 --> 00:06:36,113
Not a second sooner.

154
00:06:36,682 --> 00:06:39,990
You wouldn't pour a six-lane highway for a street with three houses.

155
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:46,807
And if part of you is thinking sure, but this is just a toy example, it really isn't.

156
00:06:47,573 --> 00:06:53,738
When Hotstar streamed the 2023 World Cup final, fifty-nine million people were watching at the very same moment.

157
00:06:54,307 --> 00:06:55,386
Fifty-nine million.

158
00:06:55,688 --> 00:06:56,454
And it held.

159
00:06:56,942 --> 00:06:59,473
The exact same request and response you just learned.

160
00:07:00,042 --> 00:07:02,805
There were simply a lot more boxes behind the scenes.

161
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:05,778
And that's it.

162
00:07:06,196 --> 00:07:07,879
I mean it, you just designed an app.

163
00:07:08,541 --> 00:07:09,783
Let's lock in what you know.

164
00:07:10,201 --> 00:07:13,324
Every app is a client, a server, and a database.

165
00:07:13,742 --> 00:07:16,471
They talk in one request, one response.

166
00:07:17,040 --> 00:07:19,187
Engineers design in four steps, every time.

167
00:07:19,756 --> 00:07:21,822
And you scale only when something breaks.

168
00:07:22,240 --> 00:07:24,435
But one question is left hanging.

169
00:07:24,922 --> 00:07:30,240
Your phone asked the server, so how did it even find that server, out of the entire internet?

170
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:34,071
That's DNS and HTTP, and that's where we pick up next time.
